<!--
  ===========================================================================
  APIM policy  |  Azure AI Foundry  |  BASIC profile
  ===========================================================================
  A cut-down sibling of wizard-foundry-policy-fixed.xml, for handing to a
  team that just wants a working OpenAI-compatible gateway in front of ONE
  Foundry account. Reference artifact: apply it by hand in the APIM portal.
  It is not wired into Bicep (the deployed policies are byok-foundry-*.xml).

  ===========================================================================
  WHAT THIS COVERS
  ===========================================================================
  Three request shapes, whichever your client sends:

      POST  /deployments/{deployment}/chat/completions   (Copilot CLI, and the
                                                          wizard import shape)
      POST  /v1/chat/completions                         (VS Code, most other
                                                          OpenAI clients)
      POST  /v1/responses                                (Responses API)

  Create the operations you need, then apply this policy at the API level so
  each one inherits it. The Foundry wizard import usually creates only the
  /deployments/... form, so the /v1/... forms have to be added by hand.

  The model name is read from the URL when it is there and from the request
  body "model" field when it is not, so all three shapes behave the same.

  YOU ALSO NEED  GET /v1/models  ON THIS SAME API.
  Copilot CLI and JetBrains AI Assistant probe it to validate the connection
  and fill the model picker BEFORE sending any chat, so a missing operation
  surfaces as "Model '<name>' not found on provider ... (HTTP 404)" even
  though the deployment is perfectly healthy. VS Code Custom Endpoint does
  not probe it, which is why VS Code can work where the CLI fails. It needs
  its OWN operation scoped policy, because the API level policy below parses
  a request body that a GET does not have. See the companion file
  wizard-foundry-policy-basic-models.xml.

  ===========================================================================
  POINTING GITHUB COPILOT CLI AT IT
  ===========================================================================
      COPILOT_PROVIDER_TYPE=azure
      COPILOT_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=https://<apim-gateway-host>/openai
      COPILOT_PROVIDER_API_KEY=<APIM subscription key>

  Host suffix is .azure-api.net on Commercial, .azure-api.us on Government.
  Leave COPILOT_PROVIDER_WIRE_API and COPILOT_PROVIDER_BEARER_TOKEN UNSET; a
  stale bearer token takes precedence over the API key and yields a 401.

  CREATE ALL FOUR OF THESE OPERATIONS, or the CLI 404s:

      POST  /v1/responses
      POST  /v1/chat/completions
      POST  /deployments/{deployment}/chat/completions
      GET   /v1/models

  /v1/responses is NOT optional in practice. CLI 1.0.81 was observed (wire
  log, 2026-08-31) sending the RESPONSES shape against a gpt-5.1 model: the
  body carried "instructions" and "input" plus "reasoning" / "store" /
  "include", NOT "messages". So do not assume the client uses chat/completions
  just because you left COPILOT_PROVIDER_WIRE_API alone. Create all four and
  the client works whichever surface it picks.

  Why all three. The azure provider DISCARDS any path on the base URL: it
  keeps only the origin and its SDK builds the rest, so the API suffix MUST
  be "openai" and a route on any other path is unreachable from the CLI.
  WHICH chat path the SDK builds depends on COPILOT_PROVIDER_AZURE_API_VERSION:
  the default versionless v1 produces /openai/v1/chat/completions, while a
  dated api-version produces the deployment scoped
  /openai/deployments/{model}/chat/completions?api-version=...
  Creating both covers either mode, and costs nothing when one is unused.
  Separately, the CLI probes GET /v1/models BEFORE it sends any chat, so a
  missing models operation reports as
  "Model '<name>' not found on provider ... (HTTP 404)".

  The credential rides in the "api-key" header. APIM's DEFAULT subscription
  key header is "Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key", which the CLI cannot send, so the
  API must be configured with subscriptionKeyParameterNames header AND query
  set to "api-key". The Foundry import wizard does this for you; a hand-built
  API does not, and the symptom is a 401 no policy can fix, because APIM
  validates the subscription key before inbound policy runs.
  scripts/deploy-basic-foundry-gateway.ps1 sets it explicitly.

  COPILOT_PROVIDER_WIRE_API selects the chat surface: "completions" (the
  documented default) produces /openai/v1/chat/completions, "responses"
  produces /openai/v1/responses. Either way the credential stays in
  "api-key", so a plain subscription key keeps working.

  BUT do not rely on the default: on CLI 1.0.81 a gpt-5.1 model produced the
  Responses shape with the variable unset. Serve both surfaces and the
  question stops mattering.

  Do NOT reach for COPILOT_PROVIDER_TYPE=openai to get the Responses API.
  That provider sends "Authorization: Bearer <value>", NOT "api-key", so an
  APIM subscription key will 401: APIM compares the WHOLE header value
  against the key and the "Bearer " prefix never matches. Renaming the
  subscription key header does not rescue it, and neither can a policy,
  because APIM validates the subscription key BEFORE inbound policy runs. It
  is only usable with subscriptionRequired turned OFF plus a validate-jwt
  policy checking a real Entra token.

  ===========================================================================
  WHAT IT DELIBERATELY LEAVES OUT
  ===========================================================================
    * Anthropic / Claude request and response translation
    * Cross cloud "commercial model" routing to a second backend
  Both need extra backends and named values, and both add a lot of policy
  expression code. Nothing here depends on them. If you need either, start
  from wizard-foundry-policy-fixed.xml instead.

  ===========================================================================
  BEFORE YOU APPLY IT
  ===========================================================================
  1. Replace REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-FOUNDRY-BACKEND-ID below with the backend the
     wizard created (APIM  >  Backends; the name usually ends in
     "-ai-endpoint").
  2. Confirm that backend's Runtime URL ENDS IN /openai, e.g.
         https://<account>.cognitiveservices.azure.com/openai
     This policy does NO path rewriting on purpose. APIM strips the API URL
     suffix and appends the operation template to the backend URL, so with
     the /openai on the end BOTH of these land on real Foundry paths:
         <backend>/openai  +  /v1/chat/completions
         <backend>/openai  +  /deployments/{deployment}/chat/completions
     If the Runtime URL is the bare account root, every call 404s FROM
     FOUNDRY even though APIM matched the operation correctly. That 404 is
     easy to mistake for a missing operation; the APIM trace tells them
     apart by showing the resolved backend URL.
  3. Decide how the gateway authenticates to Foundry. The policy below now
     SHIPS with a managed-identity block: it strips the caller's credentials
     and mints an APIM managed-identity token. Replace
     REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-FOUNDRY-MI-AUDIENCE with
         https://cognitiveservices.azure.com   (Azure Commercial)
         https://cognitiveservices.azure.us    (Azure Government)
     and grant the APIM managed identity "Cognitive Services OpenAI User" on
     the Foundry account. A freshly granted role takes a few minutes to
     propagate and returns 401 until it does.

     If your backend is a wizard-created "Azure AI Foundry" backend it already
     authenticates itself, so delete the authentication-managed-identity line.
     KEEP the two set-header deletes either way: without them APIM forwards
     the CALLER's api-key (your APIM subscription key) upstream, and Foundry
     prefers an api-key header over a Bearer token, so it rejects the call on
     that key even when a valid managed-identity token sits beside it. The
     symptom is a 401 that survives every RBAC fix you try.
  4. Sanity check the tokens-per-minute ceiling for your deployment size.

  ===========================================================================
  WHAT YOU GET IN APPLICATION INSIGHTS
  ===========================================================================
  * copilot_byok_request  (custom metric, namespace copilot.byok)
      One count per call, ALWAYS: streaming or not, success or failure.
      This is the metric to use for "requests per developer".

  * GenAI Tokens  (the wizard's built in token metric)
      Per call prompt / completion / total counts. Populated for
      non streaming chat/completions and for /responses. For STREAMING
      chat/completions it stays empty unless you flip the switch below,
      because a streamed reply carries no usage object by default.

    Both metric families carry Subscription ID, User ID, Operation ID and
    Model, so usage can be grouped by developer and model. Model is the
    requested Foundry deployment ID from the URL or request body; it is not a
    backend-returned model version. Model replaces the redundant API ID
    dimension, so the request metric remains at APIM's five-dimension limit.

  ===========================================================================
  OPTIONAL: TOKEN COUNTS FOR STREAMING CHAT COMPLETIONS
  ===========================================================================
  A streamed reply can include a final usage chunk, but only if the caller
  sends stream_options.include_usage. Most clients do not, so this policy
  can add it. It is OFF by default because a backend on an older REST
  contract rejects the field with:
      400 Unknown parameter: 'stream_options.include_usage'

  That is a REST contract version issue, not a problem with your model. The
  field arrived in 2024-09-01-preview and exists in every later version,
  including GA 2024-10-21 and the versionless v1 surface.

  TO TURN IT ON: change value="off" to value="on" on the
  streamUsageInjection line. If you then see the 400 above, change it back.
  Nothing else is affected either way.

  ===========================================================================
  TWO BEHAVIOUR NOTES (reasoning models)
  ===========================================================================
  GPT-5.6 and later cannot combine reasoning with function tools on the Chat
  Completions API. VS Code agent requests carry function tools, and GPT-5.6
  defaults to medium reasoning even when reasoning_effort is omitted, so add
  those models to a VS Code provider with apiType "responses", a
  /openai/v1/responses URL, and reasoningEffortFormat "responses".

  Foundry's Chat Completions fallback is a TOP-LEVEL
  reasoning_effort: "none" on every request containing tools. For VS Code,
  "none" must be listed in supportsReasoningEffort for the Thinking Effort
  picker to emit it, and the format must remain "chat-completions" or the
  value does not reach Foundry in the required shape. That fallback disables
  reasoning; Responses preserves it and is the recommended fix. No APIM
  policy rewrite is needed.

  gpt-5 / o1 / o3 / o4 are reasoning models and reject any non default
  temperature, top_p, presence_penalty or frequency_penalty with
  400 unsupported_value. VS Code Copilot sends temperature 0.1 for factual
  and code tasks, so this bites on the first request against such a
  deployment. The policy strips those four fields for matching model names,
  always, because forwarding them can never succeed. Other deployments (for
  example gpt-4.1-mini) are untouched and keep whatever the client sent.

  The model name comes from the URL when the path contains
  /deployments/<name>/, and otherwise from the body "model" field, which is
  what the Copilot CLI shape and /responses both send.
  ===========================================================================
-->
<policies>
  <inbound>
    <base />
    <set-backend-service backend-id="REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-FOUNDRY-BACKEND-ID" />

    <!-- Foundry prefers an api-key header over a Bearer token, so the caller's APIM
         subscription key must not reach it. Keep these two even if you drop the line below. -->
    <set-header name="api-key" exists-action="delete" />
    <set-header name="Authorization" exists-action="delete" />
    <!-- Delete this line if the backend already carries its own credentials. See header note 3. -->
    <authentication-managed-identity resource="REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-FOUNDRY-MI-AUDIENCE" />

    <!-- Change to "on" to get token counts for streaming calls. See header. -->
    <set-variable name="streamUsageInjection" value="off" />

    <!-- Model is in the URL for deployment-scoped chat and in the body for /v1 routes. -->
    <set-variable name="deploymentName" value="@{
      var path = context.Request.OriginalUrl.Path.ToLowerInvariant();
      var match = System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex.Match(path, &quot;/deployments/([^/]+)/&quot;);
      if (match.Success) { return match.Groups[1].Value; }
      try {
        var body = context.Request.Body?.As&lt;JObject&gt;(preserveContent: true);
        if (body == null) { return &quot;unknown&quot;; }
        var model = (string)(body[&quot;model&quot;] ?? &quot;&quot;);
        return string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(model) ? &quot;unknown&quot; : model.Trim().ToLowerInvariant();
      } catch {
        return &quot;unknown&quot;;
      }
    }" />

    <set-body><![CDATA[@{
        try {
            var body = context.Request.Body?.As<JObject>(preserveContent: true);
            if (body == null) { return context.Request.Body?.As<string>(preserveContent: true); }
            var path = context.Request.OriginalUrl.Path.ToLowerInvariant();
        var model = (string)context.Variables["deploymentName"];

            if (model.StartsWith("gpt-5") || model.StartsWith("o1") || model.StartsWith("o3") || model.StartsWith("o4")) {
                body.Remove("temperature");
                body.Remove("top_p");
                body.Remove("presence_penalty");
                body.Remove("frequency_penalty");
            }

            // /responses reports usage natively, so it never needs the injection.
            if (((string)context.Variables["streamUsageInjection"]) == "on" && !path.EndsWith("/responses")) {
                var stream = body["stream"];
                if (stream != null && stream.Type == JTokenType.Boolean && (bool)stream) {
                    var opts = body["stream_options"] as JObject;
                    if (opts == null) { opts = new JObject(); body["stream_options"] = opts; }
                    opts["include_usage"] = true;
                }
            }

            return body.ToString();
        } catch {
            // Not JSON we can parse: forward it untouched.
            return context.Request.Body?.As<string>(preserveContent: true);
        }
    }]]></set-body>

        <!-- Fires once per call regardless of streaming or outcome. User ID and Model match
          the token metric below so both can be sliced by developer and deployment. -->
    <emit-metric name="copilot_byok_request" value="1" namespace="copilot.byok">
           <dimension name="Model" value="@((string)context.Variables[&quot;deploymentName&quot;])" />
      <dimension name="Subscription ID" />
      <dimension name="Product ID" />
      <dimension name="Operation ID" />
      <dimension name="User ID" />
    </emit-metric>

    <llm-emit-token-metric>
      <dimension name="Model" value="@((string)context.Variables[&quot;deploymentName&quot;])" />
      <dimension name="Client IP address" value="@(context.Request.IpAddress)" />
      <dimension name="Gateway ID" />
      <dimension name="Location" />
      <dimension name="Operation ID" />
      <dimension name="Product ID" />
      <dimension name="Subscription ID" />
      <dimension name="User ID" />
    </llm-emit-token-metric>

    <!-- 200k tpm, not the wizard's 10k: one Copilot request is routinely
         5k to 15k tokens on its own, so 10k throttles the first call. -->
    <llm-token-limit
        tokens-per-minute="200000"
        token-quota="3000000"
        token-quota-period="Monthly"
        counter-key="@(context.Subscription.Id)"
        estimate-prompt-tokens="true"
        remaining-tokens-header-name="x-remaining-tpm-tokens"
        remaining-quota-tokens-header-name="x-remaining-monthly-tokens"
        tokens-consumed-header-name="x-consumed-tokens" />
  </inbound>
  <backend>
    <base />
  </backend>
  <outbound>
    <base />
  </outbound>
  <on-error>
    <base />
  </on-error>
</policies>
